WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:02.300
Vision-language models.

2
00:00:02.600 --> 00:00:05.057
They go beyond matching images to text.

3
00:00:05.057 --> 00:00:08.333
They let a language model reason about what it sees.

4
00:00:09.533 --> 00:00:11.433
The architecture has three parts.

5
00:00:11.733 --> 00:00:13.033
An image comes in.

6
00:00:13.333 --> 00:00:17.567
A vision encoder, usually a ViT, turns it into patch embeddings.

7
00:00:17.867 --> 00:00:22.533
A small projector maps those into the LLM's token embedding space.

8
00:00:22.833 --> 00:00:27.707
Now those image tokens sit in the sequence right beside the text tokens.

9
00:00:27.707 --> 00:00:31.633
To the LLM, the image is just more context to attend over.

10
00:00:32.833 --> 00:00:37.267
So it can describe, compare, extract, and answer questions about what it sees.

11
00:00:37.567 --> 00:00:40.000
Here we send a chart and ask for its data.

12
00:00:40.300 --> 00:00:42.300
And it answers with structured JSON.

13
00:00:43.500 --> 00:00:51.840
Open models like LLaVA and Qwen-VL document this recipe, while GPT-4o and Claude keep their internals unpublished.

14
00:00:51.840 --> 00:00:54.767
In production it shines in three places.

15
00:00:55.067 --> 00:00:58.000
Screenshot parsing, for UI testing agents.

16
00:00:58.300 --> 00:01:02.700
Document extraction, turning scanned PDFs into structured data.

17
00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:04.100
And chart reading.

18
00:01:05.300 --> 00:01:08.367
In code, an image is just another content part.

19
00:01:08.667 --> 00:01:10.567
One part holds the instruction.

20
00:01:10.867 --> 00:01:14.267
Another carries the image, encoded as a data URL.

21
00:01:14.567 --> 00:01:16.900
And we request a JSON object back.

22
00:01:18.100 --> 00:01:19.333
Here's a real use.

23
00:01:19.633 --> 00:01:26.133
Your corpus has scanned nineteen seventies Apollo reports, and a text extractor mangles their tables.

24
00:01:26.433 --> 00:01:31.078
You route those pages through a VLM and validate the JSON with Pydantic.

25
00:01:31.078 --> 00:01:33.400
About ninety percent come out right.

26
00:01:33.700 --> 00:01:36.541
For the rest you build a forty page eval set.

27
00:01:36.541 --> 00:01:42.033
The failures are log-scale charts and merged cells, which get flagged for human review.

28
00:01:43.233 --> 00:01:44.762
But there's a caveat.

29
00:01:44.762 --> 00:01:46.655
VLMs interpolate visually.

30
00:01:46.655 --> 00:01:53.133
They misread precise values on dense axes, hallucinate table cells, and miscount objects.

31
00:01:53.433 --> 00:01:56.951
So go find the charts it gets wrong, and evaluate them.

32
00:01:56.951 --> 00:01:58.933
The eval topics apply verbatim.

33
00:02:00.133 --> 00:02:00.975
Let's recap.

34
00:02:00.975 --> 00:02:04.833
A vision encoder and projector turn images into tokens.

35
00:02:05.133 --> 00:02:08.067
The LLM attends over image and text together.

36
00:02:08.367 --> 00:02:11.100
It's great at screenshots, documents, and charts.

37
00:02:11.400 --> 00:02:14.327
But know the failure modes, and evaluate them.

38
00:02:14.327 --> 00:02:16.300
Next up: graph neural networks.
